--- !u!114 &-1399696514247933978
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: 'Take a moment to adjust your Quality level for a smooth tutorial experience.


      1. Head to <b>Edit</b> > <b>Project Settings</b> > <b>Quality.</b>


      2. At the top of the panel, select either <b>PC High</b> or <b>PC Low.</b>


      Based on your machine''s GPU capabilities, you may choose <b>PC High</b> if you have a dedicated GPU. <b>PC Low</b> may be recommended for integrated GPUs and lower end laptops.


      For the following pages of this tutorial, we will base the instructions on the <b>PC High</b> Quality level.



      <b>Expert tips</b>


      To learn more about how Quality levels are created and set per platform, consult the following <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/2022.3/Documentation/Manual/class-QualitySettings.html">documentation.</a>

'
